                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "tag": "relative_non_defining",
                    "title": "Non-defining relative clauses",
                    "explanation": "Las oraciones de relativo explicativas añaden información extra sobre algo que ya está identificado. Van entre comas y:\n- no admiten «that»: «Harbour Studio, which I started…» (nunca «, that I started»)\n- no se puede omitir el pronombre.\n\n«Which» puede referirse a toda la frase anterior (lo cual):\n- «You want your own studio, which doesn't surprise me.»\n\nCon cuantificadores: «most of whom» (personas), «some of which» (cosas):\n- «our clients, most of whom ask for you by name»\n\nSon muy frecuentes en registro formal y escrito.",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "Uso",
                        "Ejemplo"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "who (personas)",
                          "My husband, who has been very patient, wants to travel."
                        ],
                        [
                          "which (cosas)",
                          "Harbour Studio, which I started in my kitchen, is going to change."
                        ],
                        [
                          "whose (posesión)",
                          "Mark Reed, whose company wants to buy us, made an offer."
                        ],
                        [
                          "which (toda la frase anterior)",
                          "You want your own studio, which doesn't surprise me."
                        ],
                        [
                          "most of whom / some of which",
                          "our clients, most of whom ask for you by name"
                        ],
                        [
                          "¡Nunca that ni omisión!",
                          "✗ Harbour Studio, that I started…"
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "Harbour Studio, which I started twenty years ago in my kitchen, is going to change.",
                      "My husband, who has been very patient, wants us to travel.",
                      "I've had an offer from Mark Reed, whose company wants to buy us.",
                      "I'd like you to take over some of our clients, most of whom already ask for you by name."
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "tag": "intensifier_collocations",
                    "title": "Intensifiers and collocations: absolutely delighted, highly unlikely",
                    "explanation": "Los adjetivos extremos («delighted», «exhausted», «amazing», «terrified», «furious», «impossible») no admiten «very»: se refuerzan con «absolutely», «totally» o «completely»:\n- «I'm absolutely delighted.» (nunca «very delighted»)\n\nLos adjetivos normales («happy», «tired», «good», «surprised») van con «very», «really», «extremely» o «incredibly»:\n- «I'm extremely happy.»\n\nAlgunas parejas (colocaciones) suenan naturales y otras no:\n- «highly» + «likely» / «unlikely» / «recommended» / «successful»: «It's highly unlikely.»\n- «deeply» + «moved» / «grateful» / «disappointed»: «I'm deeply grateful.»\n- «bitterly» + «disappointed» / «cold»\n- «completely» / «totally» + «different» / «wrong» / «exhausted»\n\n«Really» vale para los dos tipos: «really happy», «really delighted».",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "Tipo de adjetivo",
                        "Intensificador",
                        "Ejemplo"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "Extremo",
                          "absolutely, totally, completely",
                          "absolutely delighted, totally exhausted"
                        ],
                        [
                          "Normal",
                          "very, extremely, incredibly",
                          "extremely happy, incredibly busy"
                        ],
                        [
                          "Los dos",
                          "really",
                          "really happy, really amazing"
                        ],
                        [
                          "Colocación",
                          "highly, deeply, bitterly",
                          "highly unlikely, deeply grateful"
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "Helen, I'm absolutely delighted for you.",
                      "It's highly unlikely that Mark will give up.",
                      "I'm deeply grateful for everything you've taught me.",
                      "Lucía was completely speechless."
                    ]
                  }
